WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.160
Chris Marlow: So I'm glad

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:14.880
Jason has got some fans here.

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:19.240
I too met him
fairly recently in Oxford last year.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:24.920
And just picking up from what Sophia
was saying earlier on about representation

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.400
matters, I think he is a perfect

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:32.080
example isn't he of

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:34.600
an emerging black

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:40.040
British scholar
that one hopes will be a role model

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:43.480
and a signal for change.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:46.080
So he made history

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:50.520
earlier this year
when he was appointed as Professor

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:54.240
of Sociology of Education at Cambridge,

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:57.240
and he was filling some very big shoes

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:02.760
because the predecessor was Diane Reay,
who I'm sure

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:05.480
many of you know.

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:09.520
But I think what makes it
all the more remarkable in Jason's

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.520
case is his

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:15.480
history, his life history.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:17.600
He was born in 1985,

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:21.840
so he's a relatively young professor.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:24.480
He's I think the fourth or fifth

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:29.080
black professor at Cambridge,
but he's the youngest.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:32.160
He was born in modest circumstances,

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:35.160
a council estate in Clapham.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:38.040
And more significant, I think, is that

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:41.040
he was diagnosed as autistic

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:45.040
at three years old, at three years of age,

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.640
and also because of what I think

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.960
is termed global development delay.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:53.720
didn't learn to speak

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:56.040
until he was 11

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.280
and then didn't

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:02.480
learn to write until he was 18.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:05.760
And he had, beyond that

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:09.480
a meteoric rise through the academy.

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:11.520
He was,

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:13.600
I think, at Surrey

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:16.600
University, did a B.A. and an M.A.

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:19.680
in Education,
a PGCE, trained as a teacher,

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:25.040
as a PE teacher,
and then went on to an M.Ed and a PhD

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:29.920
at Liverpool John Moores University.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.120
Apart from his appointment at Cambridge,

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:37.480
he's also a visiting professor
at Nelson Mandela University

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:43.560
in South Africa,
and his work principally sits

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:47.120
in, I suppose, what you might call

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:50.880
decolonizing the academy.

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:55.080
I'm just looking
at some of his interests here

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:58.160
and his key publication,

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:01.320
and the one that brought him to
my attention

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:04.080
was the 2018

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:09.160
book, Dismantling Race in Higher Education,

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:12.120
subtitled Racism, Whiteness

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:15.120
and Decolonizing the Academy.

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:18.720
And that was edited with Heidi Mirza.

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:22.680
And it's a fascinating, really varied

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:26.040
edited collection, very intersectional.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:29.040
So it draws on black studies, disability,

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:32.080
race, social class, mobility.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:35.800
And I think and I'm sure colleagues

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:39.360
will have mentioned this before,

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:43.000
there are, there are two or three,
I think, probably really salient

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:46.160
publications that that gave impetus

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:49.880
to this body of work and to the

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:54.040
decolonization within the Academy,
certainly within HE.

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:57.840
And I think I would argue
that this was certainly one of them.

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:02.800
A few key points from it.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:04.200
He uses

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:09.120
the acronym BAME, and I know
that's not necessarily current these days,

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:14.480
but he says, for example, it's argued BAME
students are less likely to be admitted

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:19.880
to elite Russell Group universities
than white applicants with similar grades

00:04:19.960 --> 00:04:23.560
you know and asked the question, why?
What's going on here?

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:28.120
What are the systemic barriers to that?

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:33.000
He talks very eloquently,
very persuasive, and very personally

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:38.600
about the mistreatment
within the academy of scholars of colour,

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:43.280
citing things like unfair workloads,
precarious contracts,

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:45.920
racial microaggressions.

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:48.760
And the sense

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:52.440
that that builds, that cumulatively that builds

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:56.560
to othering, that they are,

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:00.320
they define themselves as outsiders.

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:03.520
And it's that imposter syndrome.

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:06.760
More positively, I think he talks about,

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:10.120
and I quote, the irresistible rise

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:12.960
of decolonizing movements

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:17.080
to disrupt Western epistemologies.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:21.040
I particularly want to flag this up because
this is where it's really,

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:24.400
really strong, where he and indeed

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:29.320
the other contributors are very, very keen

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:31.960
to deploy students

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:36.000
and include students as agents of change

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:42.000
and two particular initiatives,
two particular questions are raised.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:45.880
Number one, why is my curriculum white?

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:51.640
That's the question
that students are encouraged to ask.

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:56.440
And the other one,
why isn't my professor black?

00:05:56.520 --> 00:05:59.280
And I think those two questions
so salient,

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:02.720
so powerful, so potent.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:06.440
And just one more little thing here.

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:10.320
And this isn't from Jason's work within the book,

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:14.080
this is actually Sara Ahmed in chapter 19,

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:17.680
and she talks about EDI
practices in academia

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:21.120
and she quotes,

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:26.160
sorry,
my quote is: tick box exercises, that's

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:30.720
how she describes them,
to create a happy impression,

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:36.680
but actually no long lasting changes
for BAME staff.

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:40.800
I hope, I mean that was written in 2018.

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:43.360
I hope that that's perhaps no longer the case

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:47.360
given the work that's going on

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:51.840
and I just thought I'd finish
with a great quotation from the book.

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:58.680
It's not actually from Jason,
it's from Audre Lorde, 2007

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:00.280
and it's this.

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:02.600
For the master's tools,

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:08.000
we will never dismantle
the master's house.

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:12.760
They may allow us to temporarily
beat him at his own games,

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:18.760
but they will never enable us
to bring about genuine change.

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:21.640
And I think what Jason

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:24.880
typifies, what he exemplifies

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:30.640
is that desire for change and is,

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:33.720
I think, probably a real beacon

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:37.360
of hope in and beyond the academy.

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:42.520
I think what he's giving us with his work
are the theoretical

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:45.480
and the conceptual tools

00:07:45.480 --> 00:07:48.080
to challenge the status quo

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:52.760
and the fact that he is a first rate
scholar,

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:56.440
that he has social justice at his

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:59.160
at the heart of his work

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:01.840
is just brilliant.

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:04.680
And I would love if we could

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:08.560
get him to Lancaster
one way or another by hook or by crook.

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:12.960
I think it would be brilliant
to have him in the department perhaps

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:15.960
running a seminar or something similar,

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:19.520
but that's my quick take on Jason Arday.

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:21.320
I hope you've found it interesting.

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:22.120
Thank you very much.

